      "text": "The solution can be obtained by recognizing that $4^4 \\cdot 5^4$ is the square of a perfect square, which is $4^8$ or $65536$. To see why this is true, notice that $4^4 \\cdot 5^4$ can be rewritten as $(4 \\cdot 5)^4$, which simplifies to $20^4$. Since $20^4 = (2^2 \\cdot 5)^4 = 4^4 \\cdot 5^4$, it follows that $4^4 \\cdot 5^4 = 4^8 = 65536.$\n\nWhat was the denominator of Ron's probability after fishing 6 times, given that his success rate had been 1 in 10 the previous 5 times? However, after this, ten more successes followed his initial five failures. Finding for the question:\n\na) The probability of Ron's success then:\n\nP(success) = (\n    1 / 10)^ ( 5 - 1 + 10 )\n\nb) The updated probability of success after 6 trials:\nP(success) = (\n    1 / 10)^5 *(\n    10 / 15 )\n\n# Let's calculate the new probability of success after the ten more successes\n\nnew_probability_success = (\n    1 / 10)**10\n\nprint(new_probability_success)",
